---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
// TODO: replace href="#" with real affiliate links once EaseUS / Disk Drill accounts approved.
---
<Base
  title="Recover Files from a Dead or Corrupt USB Drive | Save My Gig"
  description="Your USB died with your music on it. Here's the recovery route: which software actually works, when it can't help, and when a lab is worth it."
  path="/recovery"
>
  <main class="wrap">
    <div class="status-line red">Data_Recovery</div>

    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1rem">GET YOUR <span class="accent">FILES BACK</span></h1>
    <p class="dim" style="margin-bottom:1rem">In our experience, software recovers files in most cases where the drive still powers on. It can't help a physically dead drive. Here's the route, in order.</p>

    <div class="panel panel-danger">
      <p style="margin:0"><Icon name="warn" class="danger" /> <strong>Before anything: stop writing to the drive.</strong> Don't format it, don't "try one more export". Every write overwrites recoverable music.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem">Step 1 - Recovery software</h2>
      <p class="dim">If your computer detects the drive (even showing 0 files or asking to format - <strong>say no</strong>), software has a real chance. Both of these scan free first, so you see what's recoverable before paying.</p>
      <p class="dim" style="font-size:0.85rem">These links may earn us a commission at no extra cost to you. It never changes what we recommend, and there are no paid links inside Emergency Mode.</p>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="font-size:1rem">Disk Drill</h3>
          <p>Cleanest interface, great at FAT32 recovery, Mac and Windows. Free scan shows everything first.</p>
          <a href="https://www.cleverfiles.com/disk-drill.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored" class="btn btn-go" data-event="affiliate_click" data-program="diskdrill" aria-label="Scan for free with Disk Drill" data-placement="recovery_1">Scan for free →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="font-size:1rem">EaseUS Data Recovery</h3>
          <p>The veteran. Strong on corrupt partitions and drives that ask to be formatted.</p>
          <a href="https://www.easeus.com/datarecoverywizard/" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored" class="btn btn-go" data-event="affiliate_click" data-program="easeus" aria-label="Scan for free with EaseUS Data Recovery" data-placement="recovery_2">Scan for free →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel" id="lab">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem">Step 2 - When software can't do it</h2>
      <p class="dim" style="margin-bottom:0">Drive not detected anywhere, no light, physical damage, water: that's hardware. A recovery lab opens the drive and reads the memory chips directly. It costs real money, quoted per job, so it's for irreplaceable files, your own unreleased music, not tracks you can re-download. Search for a data recovery lab in your city and ask for a free diagnosis first; serious labs quote before they charge.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem">Step 3 - Never pay for recovery twice</h2>
      <p class="dim">Recovery is what you do once. A backup system is what you do instead, it takes one evening to set up.</p>
      <a href="/prepare" class="btn btn-neutral" data-event="step_completed" data-step="prepare_click" data-placement="recovery"><Icon name="shield" /> Set up the prevention system</a>
      {/* The doctrine is the button; the checklist is the thing you actually
          do on the way out of the door. Someone who has just lost a drive is
          the most receptive reader the checklist will ever have. */}
      <p class="dim" style="font-size:0.86rem;margin:0.9rem 0 0">
        <a href="/checklist">Run the pre-gig checklist next time</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
